<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (object == null && this.get(i) == null) || (object != null && object.equals(this.get(i)));</code>

However, the comment specifies the condition for a valid index and the relationship between the added object and the elements at that index. Given the context, it seems more appropriate to focus on the last added element rather than all elements. Here's a more precise assertion based on the comment:

<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (object == null && this.get(i) == null) || (object != null && object.equals(this.get(i))) || i != this.size() - 1;</code>

But since the comment specifically mentions the index being valid and the relationship with the added object, we can simplify it to check the last added element:

<code>assert (object == null && this.get(this.size() - 1) == null) || (object != null && object.equals(this.get(this.size() - 1)));</code>

This assertion checks that if the object is null, the last element added is also null, or if the object is not null, it equals the last element added.
